            'body_en' => "The diplomat was Max von Oppenheim, and when he came back in 1911 — having left the foreign service and spent his family's banking fortune on the dig — he brought five archaeologists and more than five hundred workers. What they found was two cities one on top of the other.\n\nThe lower one was a village of about 6100 to 5400 BC, with round houses and an extraordinary pottery: thin, hard-fired, painted in red, black and white with geometric patterns and animals, among the oldest painted pottery then known. Because Tell Halaf was where it was first properly excavated, the whole culture — spread across northern Mesopotamia from the Zagros to the Euphrates, with the earliest stamp seals in the Near East — is still called Halaf.\n\nThe upper one was Guzana, capital of the Aramaean kingdom of Bit Bahiani in the tenth and ninth centuries BC. Its ruler Kapara built a palace in the hilani style — a porch with columns between two towers — and faced it with basalt. The porch columns were three colossal gods standing on a lion, a lioness and a bull; the lower walls were lined with nearly two hundred carved slabs, orthostats, of hunters, warriors, monsters and animals. In 808 BC the city became an Assyrian province, and the biblical Gozan of the deportations is almost certainly this place.",
